\n1 |RWF0|    |BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d5-d4, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, N: e4-c3, N: e4-c5, N: e4-d2, N: e4-d6, N: e4-f2, N: e4-f6, N: e4-g3, N: e4-g5, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, K: e8-d7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is in the early-mid game stage. White has developed a knight to c3 and another to f3 (implied by N on e4 being from f3), while I have a knight already active on e4, putting pressure on the center. My pieces are mostly undeveloped, especially on the queenside and king's flank. The pawn on d5 is isolated and potentially vulnerable if not supported. There is an opportunity for development and central control.\n\nKey observations:\n- My knight on e4 is well-placed, attacking key central squares and potentially threatening f2 or d2.\n- White’s knight on c3 is unprotected — if I can exchange knights favorably or create threats, it might be beneficial.\n- My light-squared bishop on c8 is blocked in and needs to be developed; possible routes include b7, d7, or e6.\n- Pawns on a7, b7, c7, e7, f7, g7, h7 are still on starting ranks and can advance.\n- Castling hasn't occurred yet for either side; however, my king is still in the center, so I should consider king safety soon.\n- White has no immediate threats, but could play moves like Nxe4 or prepare f3/Nf3/Qf3-type ideas.\n\nEvaluation of promising moves:\n1. **N: e4-c5** – Attacks the undefended knight on c3. This is a strong tactical move, winning material immediately since Nxc3 would be forced (if not defended). Very attractive.\n2. **N: e4-d2** – Forks or attacks the white queen if it's on d1? But Qd1 is not currently under attack, and after Kxd2, I lose the knight. Risky without follow-up.\n3. **P: d5-d4** – Gains space and supports the e4 knight, but may overextend. Also blocks potential retreat paths for the knight.\n4. **Q: d8-d7** – Develops queen with protection, connects rooks indirectly, and defends the e4 knight. Solid developing move.\n5. **B: c8-g4** – Developing the bishop with pin potential on Nf3 (but White doesn’t have a knight on f3 — only one on c3 and e4). Not useful here.\n6. **N: b8-c6** – Natural developing move, supports d5, prepares e5 push. Good positional choice.\n7. **P: e7-e5** – Claims center, opens bishop, but may weaken d5 further unless supported.\n\nBest among these: **N: e4-c5**, because it wins material — White’s knight on c3 is undefended, and capturing it with Nxc3 gives me a free piece. No immediate retaliation exists because the knight on e4 was protected by the queen on d8 (via d8-d7 or just holding diagonally).\n\nAfter **N: e4-c5**, if White responds with b2-b3? Then Nc3 is still hanging. Only way to save it is Nb1 or Nd1, which are passive and waste time.\n\nReason: Winning a knight for free is decisive in the opening. It maximizes my advantage and disrupts White’s development.\n\nAnswer: N: e4-c5"}
