12_23_15_28_07	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as Black.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|    |BBF0|    |KBF0|BBF0|    |RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|    |    |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |NBF1|    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |    |PBF1|    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |QWF1|    |    |PBF1|    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |NWF1|    |PWF1|PWF1|    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |    |BWF1|PWF0|PWF0|    |    |PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|    |    |    |KWF1|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, R: a8-b8, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d4-c3, P: d4-e3, P: d4-d3, P: e5-e4, K: e8-d7, K: e8-d8, K: e8-e7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is in a relatively open position with several pawn advances from both sides. My king on e8 is currently safe, though the center is partially contested. White has a queen on a4 (QWF1), which is actively placed and could pose threats along the 4th rank or diagonally. My knight on c6 (NBF1) is developed and exerts pressure on key central squares. Several of my pawns have mobility, particularly on the queenside and center.\n\nWhite’s pawn structure shows weaknesses: the d4 pawn is isolated and unsupported directly, and the white bishop on b2 (BWF1) is somewhat passive. The white queen on a4 may be vulnerable to pawn pushes like b5 or even capture if not defended properly. Additionally, White’s king has already moved (KWF1), so castling rights are lost, potentially making the king less safe in the long run.\n\nMy immediate opportunities include advancing pawns to gain space, developing pieces toward the center, or preparing to challenge White’s queen.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: a7-a6 – Prepares for potential b5 push, supports future knight or bishop development, but slow.\n- P: a7-a5 – More aggressive, gains space, pressures b4, possibly prepares rook lift or queenside expansion.\n- P: b7-b6 – Not available; would support c5 or bishop development, but not listed.\n- P: b7-b5 – Very strong: immediately challenges White’s queen on a4, forcing it to retreat or trade under favorable terms.\n- B: c8-d7 / B: f8 moves – Developing bishops, but somewhat passive right now; tempo loss possible.\n- P: d4-c3 – Captures a pawn! This is highly attractive: removes a central pawn, opens lines, and threatens the bishop on b2.\n- K: e8-e7/d8/d7 – King moves are generally unnecessary early and waste tempo unless escaping threat; no immediate danger, so likely poor choice.\n- R: a8-b8 – Prepares to double rooks or support b5 push; reasonable follow-up after b5.\n- Other pawn moves (f7-f6, g7-g6, etc.) – Less urgent; weaken kingside structure without clear benefit.\n\nKey observation: P: d4-c3 is a capture that wins material (a pawn) and disrupts White’s structure. After d4xc3, White must recapture with either Bb2xc3 or b2xc3. In either case:\n- If Bb2xc3, then ...Ba6 can pin the knight on c3 (if Nc3) or simply increase pressure.\n- More importantly, the white queen on a4 becomes extremely exposed. It has no escape square and will be captured after ...c3xb2 or ...c3-b2 followed by rook or queen pickup.\n\nWait — actually, **d4-c3** means *my* pawn on d4 captures *White’s* pawn on c3? But according to the board:\n\n- Black has a pawn on d4? Wait — let's recheck.\n\nLooking at the board:\n\n- Row 4: QWF1 (white queen on a4), then d4 has PBF1 — yes, **Black has a pawn on d4**.\n\n- Row 3: c3 has NWF1 (white knight), d4 is occupied by **PBF1** (Black pawn), e3 has PWF1 (White pawn), etc.\n\nSo Black pawn on d4, White pawn on c3? No — c3 has a white knight (NWF1), not a pawn.\n\nWait — valid move list says: **P: d4-c3**\n\nBut c3 has a white knight (NWF1), so capturing there would be **d4xc3**, capturing the knight!\n\nThat’s a critical point.\n\nSo **P: d4-c3** is a **pawn capture of the white knight on c3**!\n\nThat wins material — a knight — for free?\n\nLet’s confirm:\n\n- d4: PBF1 → Black pawn\n- c3: NWF1 → White knight\n- Is c3 adjacent diagonally to d4? Yes: d4 to c3 is a diagonal backward-left move — but pawns capture diagonally forward.\n\nDirection matters.\n\nBlack pawns move **downward** (from rank 7→8). So from d4 (rank 4), a Black pawn moves toward rank 3, 2, 1.\n\nThus, **forward** for Black is decreasing rank number.\n\nA Black pawn on d4 can capture **diagonally down-left or down-right**: i.e., **c3** or **e3**.\n\nYes — so d4-c3 is a **capture** of whatever is on c3.\n\nAnd c3 has **NWF1** — a White knight.\n\nSo **P: d4-c3** captures a knight — material gain!\n\nAnd is this protected? Does White recapture?\n\nAfter d4xc3, White can recapture with b2-b3? Or with Bb2?\n\nb2 has BWF1 — a white bishop.\n\nSo Bb2 can recapture on c3 if needed.\n\nBut still — trading a pawn for a knight is winning for Black.\n\nEven if White recaptures with the bishop, we’ve traded a pawn for a knight — **we gain ~2 pawns in value**.\n\nMoreover, White’s bishop was more active than the knight — so this might also be positionally good.\n\nAdditionally, this removes a developed piece and disrupts coordination.\n\nNow, why isn’t White just losing a piece? Because they can recapture — but still, we win material.\n\nOther options:\n\n- **P: b7-b5** — attacks the white queen on a4. The queen has no legal moves? Let’s see:\n\nQWF1 on a4 — where can it go?\n\n- Along a-file: a1–a8 — but a5, a6, a7 blocked? a5: empty? a6: empty? a7: PBF0 — Black pawn.\n\nSo queen can go a5, a6, but not beyond.\n\n- Along 4th rank: a4 to h4 — b4 empty, c4 empty, d4: PBF1 (Black pawn), so cannot go further.\n\nSo queen can go to b4, c4.\n\nSo after b7-b5, the queen can escape to b4 or c4.\n\nBut still — forcing the queen to retreat is good, and gains time.\n\nHowever, compared to **capturing a knight for free**, this is less compelling.\n\nUnless... is **d4-c3** illegal?\n\nIs the pawn on d4 pinned?\n\nWhat is behind it? The king is on e8 — no direct line.\n\nCheck if moving the pawn exposes the king.\n\nFrom d4 to c3: diagonal capture — the pawn leaves the d4 square.\n\nIs there a discovered check possibility?\n\nThe only way is if a piece behind d4 along the diagonal is attacking our king.\n\nDiagonal from d4 to c3 is light-squared.\n\nKing on e8 — light-squared bishop? Our king is on e8, which is dark.\n\nAttacking pieces along the diagonal from c3 through d4 to e5, f6, etc.\n\nWhite has no bishop on that diagonal.\n\nWhite bishop is on b2 — which is on the a1-h8 diagonal — that’s the same color as c3-d4.\n\nb2 is light-squared — yes.\n\nBishop on b2 attacks along a1-h8 diagonal: b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8.\n\nOur king is on e8 — not on that diagonal.\n\ne8 is on d7-e8-f7 diagonal — different.\n\nSo no — the pawn on d4 is **not pinned**.\n\nTherefore, **P: d4-c3** is a legal move and captures the white knight on c3.\n\nThis wins material — a knight — for a pawn.\n\nBest move on the board.\n\nOther moves like b7-b5 are good, but this is better.\n\nWhy would I not do this?\n\nOnly if White recaptures and gains a big threat — but even then, being up a knight is huge.\n\nAfter d4xc3, White can play Bb2xc3, recapturing, but now we play ...b7-b5, attacking the queen — gaining two tempi: one for capturing knight, one for threatening queen.\n\nWe come out ahead.\n\nAlternatively, if White doesn’t recapture, we keep the knight — even better.\n\nSo this move gains material and increases pressure.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nAnswer: P: d4-c3"}
