BF1 (Black Pawn, has moved). So my pawn on b7 (PBF0) can capture on a6 or b6, but more importantly, **my pawn on b7 is on b7**, and it can go to b5 or b6.\n\nBut critically: **White has a pawn on b2 (PWF1)** — that’s a white pawn on b2? No — column b, row 2: it's labeled **PBF1**, which means **Black Pawn, has moved**. So yes, **I have a black pawn on b2**, deep in White’s territory!\n\nThat pawn on b2 is one square away from queening on b1. It can promote next move. And it's protected? Let's see: White has pawns on d4, f2, g2, h2 — none near b1. The only piece that might attack b1 is the white rook on a1 or h1? Rooks on a1 and h1 — yes, Ra1 and Rh1 are both present (RWF0). So if I play b2-b1, White can recapture with Ra1xb1. But I can **choose the promotion** — and since material is roughly equal, promoting to a queen gives me an extra queen, even if it gets captured — because then I trade a pawn for a queen vs a rook: highly favorable.\n\nAlternatively, I could promote to a knight for a fork, but let’s evaluate.\n\nMy pawn on b2 can promote to Q, N, B, or R. Best is almost always queen unless tactical motif. Capturing on a1 would be b2xa1, but that’s not necessary — b2-b1 promotes on b1.\n\nValid moves include:\n- P: b2-a1-q\n- P: b2-a1-n\n- P: b2-a1-b\n- P: b2-a1-r\n- P: b2-b1-q\n- etc.\n\nBut **b2-b1** promotes on b1, while **b2-a1** captures on a1.\n\nWhich square should I promote on?\n\n- If I play **b2-b1**, I promote on b1. White can recapture with **Ra1xb1**, trading rook for queen. That’s fine — I gain a queen for a pawn, lose it to a rook — net material gain: queen is worth ~9, rook ~5, pawn ~1 → I profit ~3 points.\n\n- If I play **b2xa1=Q**, I capture on a1, promoting and removing the rook immediately. But is the rook on a1 defended? No — Kc1 is far, and no other piece defends Ra1. So if I play b2xa1=Q, I **capture the rook outright**, promoting to queen and removing the rook. That’s **free rook + queen promotion** — massive material gain.\n\nWait — can I do that?\n\nThe pawn on b2 is on **b2**, and a1 is diagonally forward-left. But pawns capture diagonally — yes. From b2, a pawn moves forward to b1, or captures on a1 or c1.\n\nIs there a white piece on a1? Yes — RWF0, a white rook. So **b2 can capture on a1**.\n\nAnd since it reaches the last rank, it must promote.\n\nSo **P: b2-a1-q** is a valid move: pawn from b2 to a1, capturing the rook and promoting to queen.\n\nThis wins a rook immediately and gains a queen — best-case scenario.\n\nWhite cannot prevent this. After b2xa1=Q, I have a new queen and White has lost a rook for free.\n\nOther options like b2-b1 also lead to queen, but then White recaptures with rook — I get queen, lose it to rook — still up material (queen for pawn vs rook: net +4 points roughly), but **capturing the rook directly gains me a full rook extra**.\n\nBecause:\n- If I play b2-b1=q: I get queen, White plays Ra1xb1 — result: I traded pawn for queen, then lost queen to rook — net: rook for pawn.\n- If I play b2xa1=q: I capture rook and promote — result: I get queen and remove rook — net: queen for pawn (same as above?), wait — no:\n\nActually, same material outcome: in both cases, I end up with a queen on the board and White loses a rook and I lose a pawn.\n\nBut in **b2-b1=q**: after Ra1xb1, I have a queen, White has no rook on a1 — same as **b2xa1=q**, where the queen is now on a1.\n\nWait — difference is:\n- b2-b1=q: queen on b1, rook on a1 still there, then Ra1xb1 — rook moves to b1, my queen is gone, his rook is on b1.\n- b2xa1=q: queen captures rook on a1, queen is now on a1, rook is gone.\n\nSo in first case, he **survives with rook**, just moves it; in second, he **loses the rook permanently**.\n\nBut in b2-b1=q, he **must** recapture or lose the rook anyway — so either way, if he recaptures, same result: rook takes queen, rook ends on b1 or a1.\n\nBut in terms of **material**, identical: I gain a queen (temporarily), he loses a rook (permanently) — net: I am up a rook (~5 points