queenside)\n\nAdditionally, promoting to **knight** on a1 could give a fork — say, after ...Na1-c2+, forking king and rook on h1? Let’s see:\n\nAfter b2xa1=n, knight on a1 — then next move Nc2+? From a1 to c2 — yes, that’s a knight move. Then it attacks king on e1 and possibly rook on h1? c2 to e1 is two up, one right — yes, knight move. c2 to h1? five right, one down — no. So only king.\n\nBut king on e1 — Nc2+ checks the king. Is there anything protecting c2? Probably not. But White can just move or block.\n\nBut getting a check is nice, but **a queen is much stronger** than a knight. The immediate material advantage of a queen outweighs a potential fork later.\n\nPromoting to bishop or rook is underpromotion with no benefit.\n\nSo best is to promote to queen, capturing on a1.\n\nThus, **P: b2-a1-q** is the strongest move.\n\nOther moves like developing the knight or advancing pawns are slow compared to gaining a queen.\n\nEven checkmating threats are irrelevant when you can win a full rook plus promote.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: b2-a1-q — wins a rook immediately and promotes to queen, massive material gain.\n- P: b2-b1-q — similar material gain, but less forcing, queen on slightly worse square.\n- Other moves (pawn advances, knight moves) ignore the immediate tactical opportunity.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended rook on a1 with the b2 pawn while promoting to queen maximizes material gain immediately and is objectively the strongest move, giving a decisive advantage.\n\nAnswer: P: b2-a1-q"}
