pawn, so d3 is free.\n\n        Bf5-c2 is legal and attacks c2, but c2 is not critical.\n\n        But **Qd5-a2** is far more powerful: it threatens immediate mate on a1.\n\n- **K: e8-d8 / K: e8-e7** – King moves are unnecessary and waste tempo.\n\n- **B: f8-c5 / f8-b4 / f8-a3** – Developing bishop, but slower than immediate threats.\n\n- **P: h7-h6** – Prevents Ng5 or Qg7-h8+, but allows Qg7-h7? Not urgent.\n\nBest move: **Q: d5-a2** — this creates a decisive threat of Qa2-a1#. White cannot prevent this mate next move unless they move their king away or play a piece to a1 (but they have none). Even then, moving the king from g3 is awkward (to h3, f3, or h2), and it disrupts their coordination.\n\nMoreover, after Qa2, if White plays Qg7-h8+, then Kf8! blocks or avoids check, and now the queen still threatens mate. Or if White blocks with a piece, they cannot save the game from material loss or mating net.\n\nReason: Q: d5-a2 delivers a devastating threat of immediate checkmate on a1, exploiting the exposed White king and lack of back-rank defense. It forces White into a losing defensive posture.\n\nAnswer: Q: d5-a2"}
