\n1 |    |    |    |RWF1|    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, R: a8-b8, R: a8-c8, R: a8-d8, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, K: e8-d7, K: e8-d8, K: e8-e7, K: e8-c8, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: g4-f3, B: g4-h3, B: g4-f5, B: g4-e6, B: g4-d7, B: g4-c8, B: g4-h5, Q: g6-f5, Q: g6-e4, Q: g6-d3, Q: g6-c2, Q: g6-b1, Q: g6-f6, Q: g6-e6, Q: g6-d6, Q: g6-c6, Q: g6-b6, Q: g6-a6, Q: g6-g5, Q: g6-g7, Q: g6-g8, Q: g6-h5, Q: g6-h6, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is highly imbalanced. My king on e8 is currently safe, but there\'s a dangerous White pawn on f3 (PWF1) and another on e5 (PWF1), both advanced deep into my side. White has already moved their king (KWF1), so castling rights are lost for White. I still have queenside castling potential (a8 and e8 unmoved, no pieces between), though the center is open. My queen on g6 is active and exerts pressure down the g-file and diagonally. The bishop on g4 (BBF1) is also well-placed, eyeing key light squares.\n\nWhite’s rook on d1 (RWF1) is semi-active, and their dark-squared bishop on f4 (BBF1) is gone—wait, actually it\'s mine: I have a dark-squared bishop on g4. Wait—correction: BBF1 on g4 is *my* bishop (Black). So yes, my bishop on g4 is attacking toward White’s weakened kingside. But more importantly, my queen on g6 is threatening mate on g2 or h2 if supported, especially since White’s king is exposed on e2 (KWF1), with minimal defenders.\n\nCritical observation: White’s king is on e2 (KWF1)—this is *extremely* unusual and dangerous. That means White’s king never castled and is stuck in the center. This is a golden opportunity to launch a decisive attack.\n\nMy queen on g6 can go to g7 (Q: g6-g7), delivering check, and possibly leading to mate. Let’s evaluate that.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Q: g6-g7 – Checks the White king at e2. Is it legal? From g6 to g7 is one square up. Yes. After Qg7+, White must respond: King can go to f1, e1, or f2? But f2 is attacked by my bishop on g4? Bishop on g4 attacks f3, e6, etc.—does it attack f2? g4 to f2 is diagonal (g4→f3→e2), but f2 is one square off: from g4 to f2 is two left, two down — that’s a diagonal of distance √8, not aligned. No, g4 does not attack f2. So Kf2 is possible. But more importantly, after Qg7+, if Kh1 or Kg1, then Rh8-h2 invades. But I don’t have a rook on h-file ready.\n\nWait—after Qg7+, White plays Ke1? Or Kf1? But then I can follow up with Bg4-f3, attacking the king and potentially winning material.\n\nBut let’s see: Q: g6-g7+ forces the king to move. If Kf1, then Bg4-f3! threatens mate on g2. White cannot defend easily. Even if they play Qxf3, I recapture with Qxf3 and threaten Rh8-h2 or Qh3+. This could be crushing.\n\nAlternatively, Q: g6-h6 — prepares support for Rh8-h8 and potential ...Bf3 ideas without losing the queen immediately.\n\nBut Qg7 is immediate and aggressive.\n\nAnother option: Bg4-f3 directly — B: g4-f3. This attacks the White queen if it were there, but White has no queen on e2. Actually, White’s queen is gone? In row 8, we have QWF1 — wait, no: row 8 has RBF0, empty, empty, empty, KBF0, BBF0, QWF1, RBF0. QWF1? That’s a *White queen* on g8? But that can\'t be right — how did a White queen get to g8?\n\nWait — recheck the board:\n\nRow 8: a8=RBF0 (Black rook), b8=empty, c8=empty, d8=empty, e8=KBF0 (Black king), f8=BBF0 (Black bishop), g8=QWF1 (White queen?), h8=RBF0 (Black rook)\n\nThat can\'t be. A White queen on g8? Surrounded by Black pieces? How did it get there? And why hasn\'t it been captured?\n\nThis suggests a possible error in interpretation.\n\nWait — look again: "QWF1" — Queen, White, 2Step=T? No: T/F flag. QWF1 means Queen White, 2Step=F (not en passant eligible), Moved=1 (has moved). So yes, a White queen on g8.\n\nBut that makes no sense tactically — it\'s behind Black lines. Unless this is a promoted queen? But how?\n\nWait — perhaps the notation is misread. Let me re-express the board carefully:\n\nRow 8: a8: RBF0 (Black rook, never moved), b8: empty, c8: empty, d8: empty, e8: KBF0 (Black king, never moved), f8: BBF0 (Black bishop, never moved), g8: QWF1 (White queen, has moved), h8: RBF0 (Black rook, never moved)\n\nSo White queen is on g8 — which is *behind* Black’s back rank. That is only possible if it was just promoted or teleported — impossible under normal play.\n\nUnless... wait — maybe it\'s a typo? Because in row 6, we have QBF1 on g6 — that would be a Black queen.\n\nWait — row 6: QBF1 on g6? "QBF1" — Queen, Black, 2Step=F, Moved=1.\n\nAh! There it is! The **Black** queen is on g6.\n\nAnd on g8, it\'s QWF1 — **White** queen.\n\nTwo queens? Yes, possible via promotion.\n\nBut how did the White queen get to g8? It must have been promoted on g8 earlier.\n\nBut now, having a White queen on g8 is extremely vulnerable — it\'s surrounded.\n\nBut in the valid moves list, I don\'t have any capture of QWF1 — because it\'s on g8, and my pieces near it are:\n\n- f8: BBF0 (Black bishop)\n- h8: RBF0 (Black rook)\n- g6: QBF1 (Black queen)\n\nSo why isn\'t capturing the White queen on g8 an option?\n\nBecause it\'s my own move — but the White queen is on g8, and I could capture it with Qg6xg8.\n\nBut that move is **not** in the valid moves list.\n\nValid queen moves include: Q: g6-g5, Q: g6-g7, Q: g6-g8 — ah! **Q: g6-g8** is listed!\n\nYes — in the valid moves: Q: g6-g8 is present.\n\nSo I can play Qg6xg8+ — capturing the White queen on g8 with check.\n\nIs that possible?\n\nFrom g6 to g8 — that\'s two squares up — same file, vertical. Is that allowed for a queen? Yes.\n\nAnd it would be a capture and check — because after Qxg8, the Black queen checks the Black king? No — the Black king is on e8.\n\nWait — who is on e2? KWF1 — White king on e2.\n\nAfter Qg6-g8, the queen captures on g8 — what is giving check to whom?\n\nThe Black queen on g8 would be checking the White king on e2? From g8 to e2 — that\'s diagonal: g8→f7→e6→d5→c4→b3→a2 — no, not aligned.\n\ng8 to e2: file difference -2 (g to e), rank difference -6 (8 to 2). Not equal magnitude → not diagonal. Distance vertically 6, horizontally 2 — not straight line. So no check.\n\nBut why would Q: g6-g8 be a good move? Only if it captures something valuable.\n\nAnd indeed — it captures the enemy queen (White queen) on g8.\n\nSo Qg6xg8 is a free queen capture.\n\nIs the White queen really undefended?\n\nWho defends g8? White pieces:\n\n- h7: PBF0 (Black pawn) — no\n- f7: PBF0 (Black) — no\n- g7: empty\n- h8: RBF0 (Black rook) — enemy\n\nWhite’s pieces: where are they?\n\nLooking at White pawns:\n- a7: PBF0 — Black\n- b7: PBF0 — Black\n- c7: PBF0 — Black\n- d2: PWF0 (pawn at d2, unmoved)\n- e2: KWF1 (king)\n- e3: PWF1 (pawn at e3? Wait — row 3: e3: PWF1? Row 3: "P: e3"? No — row 3: "PWF1" at e3? Let\'s see:\n\nRow 3: "P: e3"? No — the board says:\nRow 3: |    |    |    |    |PWF1|PWF1|    |    | — so e3 and f3 are White pawns (PWF1).\n\nThen f2: empty, g2: empty, h2: empty.\n\nWhite king on e2.\n\nSo who defends g8? No White piece is near g8. The White king is on e2 — far away.\n\nSo the White queen on g8 is completely undefended.\n\nTherefore, Qg6xg8 captures a full queen for free.\n\nThis is a massive material gain.\n\nWhy is this possible? Probably a blunder by White — leaving a queen hanging on the back rank.\n\nSo the best move is clearly to take it.\n\nNow, confirm: is Q: g6-g8 in the valid moves? Yes — listed as: Q: g6-g8\n\nNo promotion needed — it\'s a queen moving to g8.\n\nSo Answer: Q: g6-g8\n\nBut wait — is it check? Doesn\'t matter — the move is legal.\n\nAfter Qxg8, I win a queen — going from even queens to up a queen.\n\nEven if White later promotes or counterattacks, being a queen up is almost always decisive.\n\nAlternative moves like Qg7+ are flashy but riskier and don’t gain immediate material.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to capture the undefended queen.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended White queen on g8 with Qg6xg8 wins a full queen for free, providing an overwhelming material advantage that should lead to a winning position.\n\nAnswer: Q: g6-g8'}
