\n\nBut currently, rook is on h8 — so needs time.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: g8-g4** — directly attacking g4, but if white plays g3, it’s bad for queen.\n\nBut if we play **P: f5-g4**, we force a decision.\n\nMoreover, the pawn on f5 is somewhat isolated — better to trade it actively.\n\nAnother idea: **R: d4-d5** — attacking center, but d5 is empty, and may be met with c3 or something.\n\nBut less forcing.\n\nBest move: **P: f5-g4** — active, gains space, opens lines, prepares attack.\n\nIt’s also a valid move, and not committal.\n\nCompared to Q: g8-g4 — which places queen early on g4, possibly targeted by h3 or Nh4.\n\nBut Pxg4 forces the issue.\n\nMoreover, after Pxg4, if white takes back with h3xg4, then the h-file opens for rook on h8.\n\nIf they don’t take, black has a strong pawn on g4, hard to dislodge.\n\nSo **P: f5-g4** is a strong, dynamic choice.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: f5-g4 — high activity, opens game, targets king, gains space.\n- Q: g8-g4 — premature, queen might be harassed.\n- R: d4-d5 — solid, but less aggressive.\n- B: f8-g7 — developing, but slow.\n\nReason: Capturing on g4 increases pressure on the kingside, opens lines for attack, and exploits the advanced position of White\'s g-pawn. It transitions into a favorable middlegame with initiative.\n\nAnswer: P: f5-g4'}
