n, e3 has another.\n\nNow, if black plays R: d6-d3, capturing on d3, then white can recapture with e3xd3? Yes — the e3 pawn can capture en passant? No — standard capture: e3 pawn can capture **diagonally** — but only if the rook were on d4 or f4. No — the rook is on d3, same file as d3. So when rook takes on d3, it’s a direct capture. The e3 pawn cannot recapture — pawns capture diagonally, not straight.\n\nSo only a piece on d3 can defend it — but nothing does. White has no rook or queen on d-file. So d3 pawn is **undefended**.\n\nThus, R: d6-d3 wins a clean pawn.\n\nMoreover, it increases pressure on the center and improves rook activity.\n\nAlternative: P: c5-c4 — also strong, fixes pawn chain, prepares ...b5 or ...Qc7 ideas. But doesn’t win material.\n\nTherefore, R: d6-d3 is superior.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended pawn on d3 gains material without risk and increases Black’s positional advantage.\n\nAnswer: R: d6-d3'}
