m g5 to d2 is along the **light-squared diagonal**, which runs from a8-h1. Let's map it:\n\n- g5: light square (g is odd, 5 odd → light)\n- d2: d even, 2 even → light → same color.\n\nThe a1-h8 diagonal is dark squares. So g5-d2 is on the **a8-h1 diagonal**? No — a8-h1 is from top-left to bottom-right.\n\nActually:\n- The diagonal from g5 to d2 runs: g5 → f4 → e3 → d2 → c1 → b0 → invalid. So it ends at c1.\n- This diagonal passes through: d2, e3, f4, g5 — and extends to **c1 and h6**.\n- Does it hit a1? a1 is on the opposite diagonal (a1-b2-c3-d4-e5-f6-g7-h8).\n\nSo **d2 does NOT attack a1** — it attacks **c1 and e1**, but not a1.\n\nBut what about **b4**? No.\n\nSo **B: g5-d2 does NOT give check**. It just moves to d2, attacking e1 and c1, but e1 is empty, c1 is empty.\n\nBut still — **B: g5-e3**? g5 to e3: 2 left, 2 down → e3. Diagonal: e3 → d4 → c5 → b6 → a7. Again, not hitting a1.\n\nIs **any bishop move checking the king**? Only if it lands on the **a1-h8 diagonal**.\n\nThat diagonal includes: a1, b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8.\n\nCurrent bishop on g5 — can it reach any of those?\n\n- g5 to f6: f6 is on the a1-h8 diagonal? f6: f=6th file, 6th rank → yes, f6 is on a1-h8 (difference: +5,+5).\n- So **B: g5-f6** lands on the a1-h8 diagonal.\n\nNow, does it attack a1? From f6 to a1: f6→e5→d4→c3→b2→a1 — yes, same diagonal.\n\nSo **B: g5-f6** places the bishop on f6, which lies on the long diagonal pointing directly at the white king on a1.\n\nAnd the path from f6 to a1: f6-e5-d4-c3-b2-a1 — are these squares clear?\n\n- e5: empty\n- d4: empty\n- c3: empty\n- b2: empty\n- a1: king\n\nAll clear!\n\nTherefore, **B: g5-f6** delivers **check** to the white king on a1.\n\nWhite has no way to block (no piece can interpose on the diagonal), and cannot capture the bishop (no piece attacks f6). The king cannot move to b1 because b1 is not attacked — wait, is b1 on the diagonal?\n\nFrom f6 to a1: each step decreases file and rank by 1 → so next after a1 would be nothing. b1 is not on that diagonal.\n\nDiagonals from a1:\n- a1-b2-c3-d4-e5-f6-g7-h8 → yes! Exactly.\n\nSo **b2 is on the diagonal**, but **b1 is not**.\n\nSo king on a1 can only move to **b1** (if not attacked) or **a2**.\n\n- a2: is it attacked? Not currently.\n- b1: not on diagonal, so not attacked by bishop.\n\nSo king can escape to **b1** or **a2**.\n\nBut **b1** is safe from the bishop.\n\nSo **B: g5-f6+** forces the king to move to b1 or a2, but does not win material immediately.\n\nHowever, it **exposes the white king**, creates long-term threats, and gains significant initiative.\n\nAdditionally, after king moves, we can follow up with **R: d8-d2**, pinning or threatening further damage.\n\nMoreover, the bishop on f6 is extremely well placed — controlling the central diagonal, pressuring e5, d4, etc., and supporting future attacks.\n\nCompare to other moves:\n- **B: g5-h6**: gains little, retreats activity.\n- **B: g5-e7**: passive.\n- **B: g5-f4**: active, but doesn't threaten the king directly.\n\nThus, **B: g5-f6** is the most forcing and strongest move.\n\nReason: It develops the bishop to a powerful central diagonal, delivers check, forces the enemy king into the open, disrupts White’s coordination, and sets up long-term dominance over the a1-h8 diagonal. It maximizes pressure and minimizes White’s counterplay.\n\nAnswer: B: g5-f6"}
