arget White’s center or queen-side, but White’s king is on c2 — very exposed!\n\nKing on c2 (KWF1) — that’s white king on c2, which is quite advanced and possibly vulnerable.\n\nBlack has:\n- Bishop on c3 — controls b4, a5, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8\n- Rook on d8 — can go to d2? Not directly, but d2 is occupied? c2 has KWF1 — yes, king on c2.\n\nCan we attack the king?\n\nFrom c3, bishop attacks d4, e5, etc., but not c2 — c2 is same color as c3? c3 is dark, c2 is light — no, bishop can't hit c2.\n\nIs any Black piece attacking c2? No.\n\nBut if we play **B: c3-b4**, then b4 attacks c3? No — b4 is dark square, c3 is dark — wait: b4 to c3 is diagonal? b4–c3 is downward diagonal — yes, but that’s not relevant.\n\nWait: b4 would put pressure on c5, d6, etc., but more importantly, **b4 attacks a3 and c3**, but not the king.\n\nAlternatively, **B: c3-d4** — d4 attacks c3, b2, e5, f6, g7, h8 — and importantly, **d4 is one diagonal away from c2? d4 to c2 is not diagonal** — d4-c3-b2-a1 vs c2 is offset.\n\nNo direct attack.\n\nSo White king is safe for now.\n\nBack to priority: **stop h6 from promoting**.\n\nCurrently, h6 is on the 6th rank, White to move after Black. If nothing is done, White can play h7 next, then h8=Q.\n\nBut Black moves first.\n\nSo best plan: **play R: g8-g7**, threatening Rxh6 immediately.\n\nThis forces White to either:\n\n- Move the pawn h6-h7, then Black plays Rxh7\n\n- Or sacrifice the pawn, losing it anyway\n\nEither way, the promotion threat is neutralized.\n\nAlternative: **R: g8-h8**, guarding the h-file, but less effective — allows White to play h7 safely next turn.\n\nSo **R: g8-g7** is superior.\n\nAlso, it uses a rook actively, connects with potential future actions.\n\nOther moves like advancing a- or b-pawns are too slow.\n\nBishop moves are decent for activity, but not urgent.\n\nTherefore, top candidates:\n\n- R: g8-g7 — stops promotion, gains material (at least a pawn)\n\n- B: c3-g7 — looks flashy, but ends on g7, which may conflict with rook