ntral control moves, but less urgent than capturing a pawn.\n- **K: c8-b8 / c8-c7 / c8-d7**: King moves are unnecessary and waste tempo; avoid unless escaping danger.\n- **R: d8-d7 / d8-d6 etc.**: Rook development. d8-d7 connects rooks and supports e7 or f7, but not urgent.\n- **R: g7-g8**: Could be useful if targeting the 8th rank, but no weakness there.\n- **R: g7-g5 / g7-f7 / g7-e7**: g7-f7 defends f7 or eyes the center; g7-e7 targets e4, but e4 is likely defended.\n\nCritical observation: **B: c3-f6** captures the undefended white pawn on h6 (since PWF1 on h6 implies it's on h6 and not protected). However, c3-f6 lands on f6, not h6. Wait — diagonal from c3 to h6 is c3-d4-e5-f6-g7-h8? No, that's not correct. c3 to h6 is *not* on the same diagonal. c3 is light-squared; h6 is dark-squared. So **bishop cannot go from c3 to h6**.\n\nWait — correction: c3 is a light square (file c, rank 3: c is odd, 3 is odd → dark? Let's verify:\n\n- a1 = dark (black), so:\n- a1: dark\n- b1: light\n- c1: dark\n- d1: light\n- ...\n- c3: c (odd), 3 (odd) → dark square.\n\nh6: h (even), 6 (even) → even+even = even → same as a1? a1 is dark → h8 is dark, h6: h=8th file (even), 6=rank → even-even = even → color same as a1? No: coloring alternates.\n\nActually: square color = (letter_index + number) % 2  \na=1, h=8  \nSo c3: 3 + 3 = 6 → even → light square?  \nBut standard: a1 is dark (in most boards), so if a1 = (1+1)=2 → even → defined as dark → so even = dark, odd = light?\n\nWait: better to recall — bishops stay on same color. From c3: what diagonals?\n\nc3 can go:\n- up-right: d4, e5, f6, g7, h8\n- down-left: b2, a1\n- up-left: b4, a5\n- down-right: d2, e1\n\nSo c3 to f6 is possible — and f6 is a light square. h6 is on rank 6, file h. Is h6 on the same diagonal? From c3 to h6: difference of +5 files, +3 ranks — not equal → not diagonal. So **bishop cannot reach h6**.\n\nBut wait — is there a target? What about the white pawn on h6? It's on h6, but black has no piece attacking it. So no