y a knight or queen, but none exist. Can White block? Only if a piece can go between e1 and c3 — that would be d2. Is d2 occupied? Current board: d2 is empty. Could White play something like Bd2? But they don’t have a piece there now. After Be1-c3+, White must respond immediately.\n\nSo **Be1-c3+** is a strong tactical move — it gains tempo, threatens further action, and disrupts White’s coordination.\n\nCompare to other options:\n- Advancing pawns: too slow.\n- King moves: passive.\n- Rook moves: reasonable but not urgent.\n\nBut **B: e1-c3** is an immediate threat.\n\nIs it safe? Can White capture the bishop next turn? Only if they can move a piece to c3. Currently, White has:\n- King on c2\n- Pawns on e4, f4, possibly others off-board?\nWait, initial setup: we see only limited pieces.\n\nAfter Be1-c3+, White king must move:\n- Kc2-b1, Kb3, Kd1, Kd2, Kc1, etc.\n\nKd2 would walk into potential Rd8-d8+ or worse.\n\nEven better: after Be1-c3+, if White doesn't resolve properly, Black can follow up with ...Rd8-d2+ or ...Qa5+ or similar.\n\nThus, **B: e1-c3** is a powerful, forcing move.\n\nReason: It delivers check, activates a passive piece, attacks the enemy king directly, and creates immediate threats that White must address, gaining tempo and initiative.\n\nAnswer: B: e1-c3"}
