ard (from rank 8 to rank 1). So from g4 (rank 4), a Black pawn moves to g3 (rank 3) as a forward step. So P: g4-g3 is a legal non-capturing forward move.\n\nHowever, the most critical observation is that **Black has a pawn on g4**, which is already on the 4th rank — much closer to promotion than White's e4 pawn? No: actually, from g4 (rank 4), Black needs to go to g1 to promote — 3 more moves. White’s e4 pawn is on e4 (rank 4), and needs to go to e8 — 4 moves. So **Black's g4 pawn is closer to promotion**.\n\nBut more importantly, **the g4 pawn is currently supported by no other pieces**, but its advancement should be prioritized.\n\nHowever, looking at the valid moves list:\n- P: a7-a5, a7-a6\n- P: b7-b5, b7-b6\n- K: c8-b8, c8-c7, c8-d7, c8-d8\n- P: g4-g3\n- P: h7-h5, h7-h6\n\nSo the only pawn that can advance immediately toward promotion is the **g4 pawn**, with g4-g3 being the next step.\n\nBut why are a7, b7, h7 still on starting ranks? They haven’t moved. So they can move one or two squares.\n\nBut promoting the g4 pawn is the fastest path to material gain.\n\nWait — let’s double-check coordinates:\n\n- g4: Black pawn (PBF1) — yes, already moved once (F1 → moved before), so cannot move two squares. Only g4→g3.\n\nWhite has no pawns near g-file. No piece is blocking g3. So g4-g3 is safe and advances toward queening.\n\nNow, what about the kings?\n\nMy king is on c8, White’s on c3 — same file, 5 ranks apart. Not immediately threatening. No checks. No captures possible.\n\nBut time is critical. If I don’t push my most advanced pawn, White might maneuver to capture it.\n\nBut White has only a king and a bishop on g8 — BWF1. That bishop is deep in Black’s side, but possibly trapped. It’s on g8, surrounded by no White pieces. How did it get there? But regardless, it's there.\n\nBut Black has no pieces to capture it — unless the king comes near.\n\nBut more importantly: **who is to move? Black.**\n\nThe key is **pawn race**. Let’s evaluate:\n\n- Black’s g4 pawn: to promote needs g4→g3→g2→g1 = 3 moves.\n- White’s e4 pawn: e4→e5→e6→e7→e8 = 4 moves — slower.\n- But White’s king is active (c3), while Black’s king is stuck on c8.\n\nIf I don’t push the g-pawn, White may bring king to f4 or g4 to blockade or attack it.\n\nTherefore, **pushing g4-g3 immediately gains tempi** and brings it closer.\n\nOther pawns (a7, b7, h7) are far from promotion — need 6 or 7 moves.\n\nSo g4-g3 is the only move that advances a pawn toward immediate promotion.\n\nKing moves: possibly developing, but with a pawn so close to queening, unless it’s under threat, we should prioritize promotion.\n\nIs g4 under threat? Currently, no White pieces attack g4. e4 pawn doesn’t, bishop on g8 is on wrong color (g8 is dark square; g4 is also dark? g-file, rank 4: g4 = black square (since a1 is dark, g1 is light, g2 dark, g3 light, g4 dark). Bishop on g8: g8 is dark square, so same color. Can it move? From g8, it could go to h7 or f7, etc., but not to g4 — too far, and blocked by nothing? But g8 to g4 is vertical — but bishops move diagonally, not vertically. So g8 to g4 is straight line — not diagonal. So bishop cannot reach g4. Only rook or queen could, but White has none. King would take many moves.\n\nSo g4 pawn is safe for now.\n\nThus, best to push it.\n\nBut wait — is g3 attacked? After g4-g3, will it be safe?\n\ng3 is a light square. White bishop on g8 is on dark square — so controls dark squares, not light. So cannot attack g3.\n\nWhite king is far away.\n\nSo g3 is safe.\n\nMoreover, after g3, next move g2, then g1=Q.\n\nSo pushing g4-g3 is strong.\n\nNow, consider alternatives:\n\n- Moving a7 or b7 pawns: wastes time; they’re far behind.\n- h7-h5 or h6: same — slow.\n- King moves: maybe useful long-term, but pawn promotion is urgent.\n\nAlso, note that **if I don’t push g4 now, White may play Ke3 or Kf4 next, threatening to capture or blockade.**\n\nSo **immediate push is best**.\n\nEven though g4-g3 seems small, it’s necessary progress.\n\nNo other move br