ck, attacking along the diagonal directly at c4. This is a discovered threat: the queen arrives with tempo, and there\'s no pawn or piece blocking. After Qc5+, White must move the king, possibly losing coordination.\n- Qg1-d4+ is also strong, same idea: direct check, forcing king movement.\n- Qg1-b6+? Less ideal — b6 is further and not directly aligned unless supported.\n- However, **Qg1-c5+** stands out: it pins the king to the edge, gains tempo, and threatens follow-up attacks with queen and possibly king coordination. Also, if White’s king retreats, we gain space and initiative.\n\nReason: The priority is to exploit the exposed White king immediately. Delaying with pawn moves or passive king shuffles allows White to consolidate or escape. The strongest move is **Qg1-c5+**, delivering check and maximum pressure. Among valid moves listed, this is available: "Q: g1-c5".\n\nAnswer: Q: g1-c5'}
