\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, K: c8-b7, K: c8-b8, K: c8-c7, K: c8-d7, K: c8-d8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is highly simplified with very few pieces remaining. My king is on c8, and two pawns are on a7 and h7. White has a king on c5 and a bishop on b3, both of which are active. The white king is centralized and advancing, while my king is still in the corner. There is no immediate threat of checkmate, but if I don’t act carefully, White may dominate the center and restrict my king. My pawns are on their starting ranks (a7 and h7), so they can move one or two squares forward.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Moving the a-pawn to a6 or a5:  \n  - a7-a6: Slow, only one square. Doesn’t gain much space or tempo.  \n  - a7-a5: Advances two squares, gains space, potentially prepares future queenside activity. However, it also commits the pawn and may become weak if unsupported. But given the lack of nearby enemy pawns, this is low risk.\n- Moving the h-pawn to h6 or h5:  \n  - h7-h6: Prevents any potential future Bg2+ or Bh1-a8 ideas by White, but otherwise passive.  \n  - h7-h5: Gains space, keeps options open for kingside expansion. Less urgent since there’s no immediate target.\n- King moves (c8-b7, b8, c7, d7, d8):  \n  - The king must eventually centralize. Currently, it's passive on c8.  \n  - c8-d7: Centralizes slightly, connects with potential pawn breaks.  \n  - c8-b7: Moves toward the center and supports a5/a6 pawn. Also gets off the back rank.  \n  - c8-d8 or c8-b8: Retreats or lateral; less useful.  \n  - c8-c7: Simple development, but b7 is better for activity.\n\nBest options:  \n1. K: c8-b7 — improves king activity, heads toward center, supports a5 pawn break, avoids passivity.  \n2. P: a7-a5 — gains space, prevents White from easily controlling queenside.  \n3. P: h7-h5 — less impactful, but could be useful later.\n\nHowever, **king inactivity is the biggest risk**. White’s king is already on c5, ready to support promotion or invade. My best plan is to activate the king immediately.\n\nReason: The most pressing need is king activity. The move K: c8-b7 develops the king toward the center, supports the a-pawn, and prepares to challenge White’s control. It’s more strategic than pawn moves, which can wait one move. In endgames, king centralization is key — delaying it loses time.\n\nAnswer: K: c8-b7"}
