\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: a6-a5, K: a6-b5, K: a6-b6, K: a6-b7, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is highly simplified with only a few pieces remaining. My king is on a6, and White’s king is on d6 — quite close, creating a potential king opposition scenario. There is a White bishop on g8, which is oddly placed but could become active if I’m not careful. White also has a pawn on e5, while I have two pawns: one on h7 (mobile) and one on a7 (blocked). The most immediate concern is king activity and trying to gain positional advantage or promote a pawn.\n\nMy valid moves are limited:\n- King moves: a6-a5, a6-b5, a6-b6, a6-b7\n- Pawn moves: h7-h5, h7-h6\n\nEvaluation:\n- K: a6-a5 — Advances the king toward the center and supports potential b-pawn advancement (though no b-pawn exists), but may not be optimal as it doesn’t directly challenge White’s structure.\n- K: a6-b5 — Centralizes the king slightly, controlling c4 and a4, possibly preparing to attack the e5 pawn or support an invasion. Stronger than a5 in flexibility.\n- K: a6-b6 — Keeps king on open file, supports potential coordination, but less aggressive.\n- K: a6-b7 — Moves toward the center and threatens to cut off the White king from the queenside. Also eyes the b-file and could support ...a5 push later. This move increases king activity and central control.\n- P: h7-h5 — A pawn move that frees the h6 square for the king or prepares ...h4 and potential promotion on the wing. However, it risks creating a weakness if not supported.\n- P: h7-h6 — Only advances one square; passive and loses tempo compared to h5.\n\nThe key insight is that this appears to be an endgame where king activity and pawn promotion races matter. My h-pawn is potentially capable of advancing toward promotion if supported. Pushing h7-h5 now gains space and opens the possibility of h4 and h3, especially since White has no pawns on that side to counter.\n\nHowever, the kings are close, and central control might allow me to pressure White’s e5 pawn. But my king is on the queenside, and moving it toward the center (e.g., ...Kb7) improves its scope.\n\nBest among options: **K: a6-b7** — it activates the king, avoids passivity, and prepares to influence the center or support future action. Alternatively, **P: h7-h5** aims for a queenside breakthrough.\n\nBut given that the White king is already centralized (d6), I should avoid wasting time. Activating my king with **K: a6-b7** improves my position and keeps multiple plans open.\n\nReason: King activation is critical in endgames. Moving Kb7 increases my king’s influence, coordinates better with potential pawn breaks, and avoids passive pawn moves that don't threaten immediate progress. It’s more flexible and dynamic than h-pawn moves at this stage.\n\nAnswer: K: a6-b7"}
