\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, K: b7-a6, K: b7-a8, K: b7-b6, K: b7-b8, K: b7-c8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: As Black, I am in a highly unusual and precarious position. My king is on b7, which is extremely exposed and deep in enemy territory, surrounded by very few pieces. The white king is on d6 (KWF1), relatively close, and there is a white pawn on e5 (PWF1) that could potentially advance or support an attack. I have only two pieces left: a pawn on a7 (PBF0) and my king on b7. The white bishop is on g7 (BWF1), which may threaten key squares around my king.\n\nMy king is currently under no immediate check, but its position is very weak. Any move must carefully avoid walking into check or worsening the king’s exposure. The nearby white king could potentially close in for a mating net if I’m not careful.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: a7-a5 — Advances the pawn two squares, maintaining flexibility. Could aim to promote it eventually, but it’s still far from queening and might be too slow given the king's vulnerability.\n- P: a7-a6 — Safer pawn move, one step forward. Less ambitious, but avoids potential en passant issues later and keeps options open.\n- K: b7-a6 — Moves the king to a6, which is closer to the pawn but potentially dangerous as it blocks the pawn’s path and may not improve safety.\n- K: b7-a8 — Retreats to a8, a corner, offering no progress and reducing king activity. Likely worse as it cedes space and initiative.\n- K: b7-b6 — Approaches the center slightly, but moves toward the white king on d6, risking confrontation where White likely has better coordination.\n- K: b7-b8 — Moves to b8, staying passive and retreating without clear purpose.\n- K: b7-c8 — Centralizes slightly toward c8, possibly preparing to cover more squares or avoid zugzwang, though still passive.\n\nThe pawn moves a7-a5 or a7-a6 are more promising because they aim to create a passed pawn, which might be Black’s only winning chance in this endgame-like scenario. Advancing the pawn two squares (a7-a5) gains more ground and accelerates the push, which is often critical in king-pawn endgames.\n\nHowever, caution is needed: moving a7-a5 makes the pawn vulnerable to en passant capture *if* a white pawn were on b5—but White has no such pawn. So en passant is not a threat here. Thus, a7-a5 is safe in that regard.\n\nReason: The best chance for Black to win is to push the a-pawn as quickly as possible to promote, while the king tries to support it. a7-a5 gains maximum tempo compared to a7-a6. While the king is exposed, accelerating the pawn increases counterplay, which is essential when at a material disadvantage.\n\nAnswer: P: a7-a5"}
