y forward-progressing king move is **K: d7-d6**, which brings the king closer to the center and potentially queenside support.\n\nK: d7-d6 is the most active king move available.\n\nCompare:\n\n- Pushing the pawn now (a5-a4) loses it after Kb6xa5 — leading to draw.\n- But delaying might allow White to improve their king position.\n\nHowever, after **K: d7-d6**, you threaten to continue with Ke5, Kd4, etc., approaching the pawn.\n\nAlso, note that after Kd7-d6, if White goes Kb6-a5, you can play Kd6-c5, attacking the White king and threatening to drive it away.\n\nMoreover, with your king on d6, you prepare to march to c5, b5, and support a5-a4 at the right moment.\n\nThus, **K: d7-d6** is more useful than moving away (d8, c8, e7, e8), which lose tempo.\n\nAmong all choices, **K: d7-d6** is the only one that actively improves your king’s position toward the critical area.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: a5-a4 — loses pawn immediately if Kb6xa5; leads to draw. Premature.\n- K: d7-c8 — passive, retreats sideways.\n- K: d7-d8 — retreats, worse.\n- K: d7-e8 / e7 / e6 — all go away from the action.\n- K: d7-d6 — advances king toward center and potential support for a5 pawn.\n\nReason: Advancing the king with K: d7-d6 prepares to support the a5 pawn’s advance by cutting off the White king or contesting key squares. It increases activity and keeps the possibility of promoting the pawn alive, whereas pushing the pawn now loses it without compensation. This move maximizes winning chances by centralizing the king and preparing to challenge for opposition.\n\nAnswer: K: d7-d6"}
