 flexibility.\n\nThe best strategy in king vs king endgames is to avoid unnecessary proximity and keep mobility. Moving toward the center or away from the opponent increases freedom.\n\nAmong the options:\n- c5 brings the king closer to White (distance from b3: 2 files, same rank) — slightly risky\n- e6 is farthest and central-diagonal, offering most future mobility\n\nBut all moves are equivalent in outcome (draw). Still, to maximize theoretical winning chances (e.g., if opponent blunders into illegal position), we should avoid passive placement.\n\nBest practical choice: **d5-e6**, centralizing and maximizing distance while preserving full mobility.\n\nReason: e6 is a central and non-committal square, far from White’s king, reducing risk of accidental stalemate and maintaining maximum future move options. It avoids edge play and keeps the king active.\n\nAnswer: K: d5-e6'}
