both of which head toward the center.\n\nReason: While no move changes the theoretical result (draw), moving toward the center (e.g., K: e6-d5 or K: e6-f5) maximizes potential mobility and prepares for hypothetical scenarios if more pieces were introduced (e.g., in a real game where pawns might promote). Among these, K: e6-f5 advances toward the center-right and maintains good king activity.\n\nHowever, since all outcomes are drawn, we choose a move that keeps the king active and avoids edge placement.\n\nAnswer: K: e6-f5"}
